.TH TRUE 1 2026-10-19 "w64devkit" "w64devkit Manual"
.SH NAME
true \- do nothing, successfully
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B true
.RI [ argument ...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B true
exits with status 0.
It does nothing else: it reads no input, writes no output, and ignores
its arguments.
It does not use the standard streams, so a closed one makes no
difference.
This
.B true
is a busybox applet; see
.BR busybox (1).
.PP
Nothing on the command line is an option.
.BR \-\-help ,
.BR \-\-version ,
and
.B \-\-
are ignored like any other argument.
The shell still expands the arguments and performs the redirections
before it runs
.BR true ,
so
.BI "true >" file
creates or truncates
.IR file .
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.B true
reads no environment variables.
The startup code shared by all applets reads
.BR BB_CRITICAL_ERROR_DIALOGS ,
.BR BB_GLOBBING ,
and
.BR BB_UMASK ,
and may trim a trailing semicolon from
.BR PATH .
None of these changes what
.B true
does; see
.BR busybox (1).
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Always.
.SH EXAMPLES
Show the exit status of
.BR true :
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ true; echo $?
0
.EE
.RE
.PP
Create an empty file, or empty an existing one:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ true >empty.txt
.EE
.RE
.PP
Keep a script that uses
.B set \-e
going when a command is allowed to fail:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ set \-e
$ grep \-q error log.txt || true
.EE
.RE
.SH NOTES
Unlike the GNU
.BR true ,
this one has no
.B \-\-help
or
.B \-\-version
and never prints anything.
.B busybox \-\-help true
prints the busybox version and
.B No help available
on standard output, and exits with status 0.
.PP
In the kit's shell,
.B true
is a builtin, so typing it does not start the applet.
The shell's
.B :
builtin runs the same code.
Applets that run a command run this applet inside their own process, as
.B find \-exec
does, or start
.I busybox.exe
to run it, as
.B env
does.
.B BB_OVERRIDE_APPLETS
can make them run a program from
.B PATH
instead; see
.BR busybox (1).
From
.BR cmd.exe ,
the launcher
.I true.exe
or
.B busybox true
runs it.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR busybox (1),
.BR env (1),
.BR false (1),
.BR find (1),
.BR sh (1)
